\title{strainselect: Select Bacterial Strains to Sequence Based on Their Metadata}
\description{
Helps choosing which bacterial strains to sequence from a collection described by categorical metadata (sampling context, matrix, region, year...). Two methods are provided. The clustering method groups the strains into a chosen number of clusters using the Gower distance and hierarchical clustering, and selects the best clustered strain of each cluster according to its silhouette width; it is suited when the number of strains that can be sequenced is limited. The profile method groups the strains that share exactly the same metadata and selects one strain per group, so that every distinct profile is represented.
}
\section{Methods}{

Both methods start with \code{\link[=prepare_input]{prepare_input()}}, which cleans the table of
metadata.
\itemize{
\item The \strong{clustering method} is suited when the number of strains that can
be sequenced is limited: \code{\link[=assess_gower]{assess_gower()}} computes Gower distances and a
hierarchical clustering, \code{\link[=cstats_table]{cstats_table()}} helps choosing the number of
clusters, and \code{\link[=select_silhouette]{select_silhouette()}} selects the best clustered strain of
each cluster.
\item The \strong{profile method} is suited when the number of strains is not
limited: \code{\link[=prepare_upset]{prepare_upset()}} builds a binary table of modalities,
\code{\link[=define_profiles]{define_profiles()}} groups strictly identical strains, and
\code{\link[=select_profiles]{select_profiles()}} selects one strain per group.
}

See \code{vignette("get-started", package = "strainselect")} for a complete
example.
